<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Madrid</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Madrid is the capital of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, in the centre of the Iberian peninsula. Philip II made it the permanent seat of the royal court in 1561. From then on it was the centre of government of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>, where the councils that administered its territories were based.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/madrid.jpg" alt="Madrid" width="320" height="168" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Zarateman · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Madrid_-_Sky_Bar_360%C2%BA_(Hotel_Riu_Plaza_Espa%C3%B1a),_vistas_19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The kings of Castile ruled without a fixed capital for most of the Middle Ages, their court moving between Burgos, Toledo, Valladolid, Seville and other cities until Philip II settled it at <b>Madrid</b> in 1561.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>After the last Sforza duke, Francesco II, died in 1535, Charles V kept the duchy and invested his son Philip; Spanish governors, the Senate and the Council of Italy in <b>Madrid</b> ruled it until 1706.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-milan">Duchy of Milan · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip II (r. 1556–1598) fixed the court at <b>Madrid</b> in 1561 and governed largely on paper, annotating thousands of reports from his study, while building the palace-monastery of El Escorial (1563–1584).</blockquote><a href="/empire/habsburg-spain">Habsburg Spain · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The Spanish alliance of 1528 gave the republic stability and great banking wealth, but it was a client of <b>Madrid</b>, exposed to the repeated bankruptcies of the Spanish crown, the last of which in 1627 hit Genoese lenders hard.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-genoa">Republic of Genoa · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The 'Visit Rwanda' campaign of 2018 bought sponsorships with Arsenal, Paris Saint-Germain and Atlético <b>Madrid</b>; Kigali hosted the UCI Road World Championships in September 2025.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rwanda">Rwanda · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Corruption at every level of this complex structure was endemic, and the distances from <b>Madrid</b> to Lima or Manila meant that royal instructions often arrived months or years after the situations that prompted them had already changed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
